{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**The problem.** A network has `n` nodes, labelled `1` to `n`. Each entry `[u, v, w]` in `times` means a signal sent from `u` reaches `v` after `w` units of time (one way only). A signal starts at node `k`. Return how long it takes until **every** node has received it, or `-1` if some node never does."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/3f04410c-f921-4276-b466-ec366b99f020.svg",
      "alt": "Four nodes. From 1: an edge to 2 taking 9, an edge to 3 taking 2. From 3: to 2 taking 3, to 4 taking 8. From 2: to 4 taking 1. The fastest times from node 1, in green, are 1 to 3 (2), then 3 to 2 (total 5), then 2 to 4 (total 6). The direct edge 1 to 2 is slower than going round through 3. The answer is 6.",
      "width": 832,
      "height": 409,
