    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "times = [[2,1,1],[2,3,1],[3,4,1]], n = 4, k = 2   ->  2\ntimes = [[1,2,1]], n = 2, k = 1                   ->  1\ntimes = [[1,2,1]], n = 2, k = 2                   ->  -1   (nothing leaves node 2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: Dijkstra's algorithm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Each node hears the signal at its **shortest-path time** from `k`, so the answer is the largest of those times. The edges have **different weights**, and that breaks plain BFS: BFS assumes fewer edges means shorter, but in the picture the route with more edges (1 → 3 → 2) beats the direct one."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Dijkstra's algorithm** keeps BFS's spirit — settle the nearest things first — using **distance** instead of edge count. Keep a best-known distance for every node, and a **min-heap** of nodes ordered by that distance. Repeatedly pop the closest node: its distance is now **final**, because any other route to it would have to continue from a node that is already at least as far away, and weights are never negative. Then **relax** its edges: if going through it gives a neighbour a shorter distance, record that and push the neighbour."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two practical details. A node can be pushed more than once as its distance improves; when an **out-of-date** entry is popped later, just skip it. And Dijkstra needs weights that are **zero or more** — one negative edge can make a settled node's distance wrong. JavaScript has no built-in heap, so the code uses the small `Heap` class from the Heap course."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Bellman-Ford: relax every edge, again and again"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A simpler idea that needs no heap. Go through every edge and **relax** it: if `dist[u] + w` beats `dist[v]`, lower `dist[v]`. One pass fixes every shortest path that uses one edge; the next pass, two edges; and so on. A shortest path never needs more than `n - 1` edges, so `n - 1` passes are enough — stop early if a pass changes nothing."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function networkDelayTime(times, n, k) {\n  const dist = new Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);\n  dist[k] = 0;\n  for (let round = 1; round < n; round++) {   // a shortest path uses at most n - 1 edges\n    let changed = false;\n    for (const [u, v, w] of times) {\n      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {        // relax: a cheaper way to reach v through u\n        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n        changed = true;\n      }\n    }\n    if (!changed) break;                  // nothing improved: every distance is final\n  }\n  const slowest = Math.max(...dist.slice(1));\n  return slowest === Infinity ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def network_delay_time(times, n, k):\n    dist = [float(\"inf\")] * (n + 1)\n    dist[k] = 0\n    for _ in range(n - 1):                # a shortest path uses at most n - 1 edges\n        changed = False\n        for u, v, w in times:\n            if dist[u] + w < dist[v]:     # relax: a cheaper way to reach v through u\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + w\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            break                         # nothing improved: every distance is final\n    slowest = max(dist[1:])\n    return -1 if slowest == float(\"inf\") else slowest"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int networkDelayTime(int[][] times, int n, int k) {\n    final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    int[] dist = new int[n + 1];\n    Arrays.fill(dist, INF);\n    dist[k] = 0;\n    for (int round = 1; round < n; round++) {        // a shortest path uses at most n - 1 edges\n        boolean changed = false;\n        for (int[] t : times) {\n            int u = t[0], v = t[1], w = t[2];\n            if (dist[u] != INF && dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {   // relax: a cheaper way to reach v through u\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n                changed = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!changed) break;                         // nothing improved: every distance is final\n    }\n    int slowest = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) slowest = Math.max(slowest, dist[i]);\n    return slowest == INF ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int networkDelayTime(vector<vector<int>>& times, int n, int k) {\n    const int INF = INT_MAX;\n    vector<int> dist(n + 1, INF);\n    dist[k] = 0;\n    for (int round = 1; round < n; round++) {        // a shortest path uses at most n - 1 edges\n        bool changed = false;\n        for (auto& t : times) {\n            int u = t[0], v = t[1], w = t[2];\n            if (dist[u] != INF && dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {   // relax: a cheaper way to reach v through u\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;\n                changed = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!changed) break;                         // nothing improved: every distance is final\n    }\n    int slowest = *max_element(dist.begin() + 1, dist.end());\n    return slowest == INF ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V · E), space O(V).** Up to V - 1 passes over all E edges. Slower than Dijkstra, but it copes with **negative** weights — and the idea of counting passes returns in Pattern 9."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Dijkstra with a min-heap"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Push `[0, k]`. Pop the closest entry; skip it if it's out of date; otherwise relax each edge leaving it. When the heap is empty, every reachable node has its final time."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "network-delay",
      "caption": "The heap always hands back the closest unsettled node. Try the example where the longer route is faster."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function networkDelayTime(times, n, k) {\n  const adj = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => []);\n  for (const [u, v, w] of times) adj[u].push([v, w]);\n  const dist = new Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);\n  dist[k] = 0;\n  const heap = new Heap((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);   // [distance, node], closest first\n  heap.push([0, k]);\n  while (heap.size() > 0) {\n    const [d, u] = heap.pop();\n    if (d > dist[u]) continue;              // a stale entry: u was already reached cheaper\n    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {\n      if (d + w < dist[v]) {                // a cheaper way to reach v, through u\n        dist[v] = d + w;\n        heap.push([dist[v], v]);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  const slowest = Math.max(...dist.slice(1));\n  return slowest === Infinity ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "import heapq\n\ndef network_delay_time(times, n, k):\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for u, v, w in times:\n        adj[u].append((v, w))\n    dist = [float(\"inf\")] * (n + 1)\n    dist[k] = 0\n    heap = [(0, k)]                       # (distance, node): heapq pops the smallest\n    while heap:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if d > dist[u]:\n            continue                      # a stale entry: u was already reached cheaper\n        for v, w in adj[u]:\n            if d + w < dist[v]:           # a cheaper way to reach v, through u\n                dist[v] = d + w\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (dist[v], v))\n    slowest = max(dist[1:])\n    return -1 if slowest == float(\"inf\") else slowest"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int networkDelayTime(int[][] times, int n, int k) {\n    List<List<int[]>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (int[] t : times) adj.get(t[0]).add(new int[]{t[1], t[2]});\n    int[] dist = new int[n + 1];\n    Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);\n    dist[k] = 0;\n    PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));   // [distance, node]\n    heap.add(new int[]{0, k});\n    while (!heap.isEmpty()) {\n        int[] top = heap.poll();\n        int d = top[0], u = top[1];\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;                   // a stale entry: u was already reached cheaper\n        for (int[] e : adj.get(u)) {\n            int v = e[0], w = e[1];\n            if (d + w < dist[v]) {                   // a cheaper way to reach v, through u\n                dist[v] = d + w;\n                heap.add(new int[]{dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    int slowest = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) slowest = Math.max(slowest, dist[i]);\n    return slowest == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int networkDelayTime(vector<vector<int>>& times, int n, int k) {\n    vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> adj(n + 1);\n    for (auto& t : times) adj[t[0]].push_back({t[1], t[2]});\n    vector<int> dist(n + 1, INT_MAX);\n    dist[k] = 0;\n    priority_queue<pair<int, int>, vector<pair<int, int>>, greater<>> heap;   // (distance, node), smallest first\n    heap.push({0, k});\n    while (!heap.empty()) {\n        auto [d, u] = heap.top(); heap.pop();\n        if (d > dist[u]) continue;                   // a stale entry: u was already reached cheaper\n        for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {\n            if (d + w < dist[v]) {                   // a cheaper way to reach v, through u\n                dist[v] = d + w;\n                heap.push({dist[v], v});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    int slowest = *max_element(dist.begin() + 1, dist.end());\n    return slowest == INT_MAX ? -1 : slowest;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(E log V), space O(V + E).** Each edge can push one heap entry, and each push or pop costs O(log E) = O(log V). The adjacency list and the heap take O(V + E)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Bellman-Ford: relax every edge, again and again",
          "time": "O(V · E)",
          "timeWhy": "Up to **V − 1** passes over all **E** edges.",
          "space": "O(V)",
          "spaceWhy": "One distance **per vertex**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Dijkstra with a min-heap",
          "time": "O(E log V)",
          "timeWhy": "Each edge can **push once**, and each push or pop costs **log**.",
          "space": "O(V + E)",
          "spaceWhy": "The adjacency list and the **heap**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Use Dijkstra whenever all weights are non-negative — that's most interview questions. Reach for Bellman-Ford when weights can be negative, or when the path may use only a limited number of edges (next pattern)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**An unreachable node** — its distance stays infinite: return -1.",
        "**A single node** — `n = 1`: the signal is already everywhere, so the answer is 0.",
        "**Edges are one-way** — `[2,1,1]` lets 2 reach 1, not 1 reach 2.",
        "**Two routes of different lengths** — the one with more edges can be faster; this is exactly what plain BFS gets wrong."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Path With Minimum Effort** — Dijkstra on a grid, where a path's cost is its biggest step instead of a sum. Next lesson.",
        "**Path with Maximum Probability** — multiply instead of add, and use a max-heap: the best probability so far comes out first.",
        "**Number of Ways to Arrive at Destination** — Dijkstra that also counts, for each node, how many shortest paths reach it."
      ]
    }
  ],
